    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. You mentioned \"flow friction\" being lowered in roundabouts. Could you go a bit deeper into what actually causes that friction in a signalized system? I'm interested in the specific variables—like driver behavior or sudden fluctuations—that lead to those unpredictable disruptions in the flow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Flow friction in a signalized system is largely caused by the inherent *discontinuity* of the flow, which is a predictable yet disruptive pattern. When a light turns red, the entire stream of vehicles must abruptly decelerate, come to a complete stop, and then re-accelerate when the light changes. This stop-and-go cycle introduces significant energy loss and temporal gaps in the flow.\n\nDriver behavior exacerbates this, as the reaction time to the signal change, coupled with the need to merge back into the stream after a stop, creates localized turbulence. Sudden fluctuations, like an unexpected heavy platoon arriving just as the light turns green, can cause the queue to spill back into adjacent lanes, creating a ripple effect of congestion.\n\nThe system is designed around discrete time slots, and any deviation from the perfect timing or perfect driver adherence creates this friction, which is much more pronounced than the smooth negotiation of a roundabout. It's the *interruption* that is the primary culprit.\n\nIf you want to compare this to a roundabout, the friction there is much lower because the negotiation is continuous, not episodic.\n\nThis level of detail about flow disruption is fascinating! Do you have any other questions about traffic dynamics?",
      "source": "target",
